# Shared .env body for the Llamenos worker container — the SINGLE source of
# truth for every env var apps/worker/lib/config.ts (and the optional
# features it gates) can read.
#
# Included by BOTH deployment paths via `lookup('ansible.builtin.template', ...)`:
#   - roles/llamenos-app/templates/env/app.j2   (per-service role — production/staging)
#   - roles/llamenos/templates/env.j2           (monolithic role — demo/staging path)
#
# DO NOT duplicate this list into either of those two files again. That
# duplication is exactly how #716/#771 happened: roles/llamenos/templates/env.j2
# silently drifted from roles/llamenos-app/templates/env/app.j2 and ended up
# missing DATABASE_URL entirely, so the app container failed to boot on the
# demo/staging deploy path. If a new var needs to reach the worker container,
# add it HERE ONCE — both roles pick it up automatically.
#
# Each role may still append its OWN extra vars (e.g. DOMAIN/ACME_EMAIL in the
# monolithic role's Caddy-adjacent .env) after including this file — that's
# fine, as long as the vars the WORKER PROCESS itself reads live only here.
#
# Required-by-apps/worker/lib/config.ts vars are unconditional below
# (DATABASE_URL, HMAC_SECRET, SERVER_SECRET, HOTLINE_NAME, ENVIRONMENT,
# WEBHOOK_BASE_URL when app_environment=production) — see
# deploy/ansible/scripts/check-required-env.py, which parses config.ts and
# fails CI if any of them stop appearing in a render of either role's output.
PLATFORM=bun
PORT=3000
# Both deployment profiles put Caddy in front of this container, and the app
# port is published on 127.0.0.1 only — Caddy is the sole ingress. Caddy SETS
# X-Forwarded-For to the real remote address and deletes every other
# forwarded-for header (roles/llamenos-caddy/templates/caddy.j2), so the
# right-most X-Forwarded-For entry the app reads is authentic.
#
# Without this the app bucketed every caller in the world on Caddy's container
# address: one rate-limit bucket for the entire internet, five logins a minute
# total, and a one-client denial of service against the hotline (#1606). Do not
# set it on any host where the app port is reachable without passing through
# Caddy.
TRUST_PROXY_HEADERS=true
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://llamenos:{{ pg_password }}@postgres:5432/llamenos
PG_POOL_SIZE={{ pg_pool_size | default(10) }}
{% if (admin_pubkey | default('')) | length > 0 %}
ADMIN_PUBKEY={{ admin_pubkey }}
{% endif %}
{# ADMIN_PUBKEY is an Ed25519 SIGNING key. Admin envelopes need the admin's
   X25519 ENCRYPTION key, which is a different value derived from the same
   seed. The app no longer falls back to ADMIN_PUBKEY (#1283) — it refuses to
   start when ADMIN_PUBKEY is set and this is not, rather than write envelopes
   nobody can open. `bun run bootstrap-admin` prints both.

   `or undef(...)` rather than `| mandatory`: vars.example.yml ships
   `admin_decryption_pubkey: ""`, so the var is DEFINED on every real deploy
   and `mandatory` — which only rejects an undefined var — would pass straight
   through and emit an empty value. That renders a .env the app then refuses
   to boot on, turning a clear render-time error into a container crash loop.
   Failing on empty as well as undefined keeps the error where the operator
   can act on it. #}
{% if (admin_pubkey | default('')) | length > 0 %}
ADMIN_DECRYPTION_PUBKEY={{ (admin_decryption_pubkey | default('')) | trim or undef(hint='admin_decryption_pubkey is required whenever admin_pubkey is set — the admin X25519 HPKE recipient key, distinct from the Ed25519 admin_pubkey; run: bun run bootstrap-admin') }}
{% elif (admin_decryption_pubkey | default('')) | trim | length > 0 %}
ADMIN_DECRYPTION_PUBKEY={{ admin_decryption_pubkey | trim }}
{% endif %}
{% if (ntfy_allowed_origins | default('')) | length > 0 %}
NTFY_ALLOWED_ORIGINS={{ ntfy_allowed_origins }}
{% endif %}
HOTLINE_NAME={{ hotline_name | default('Hotline') }}
{#
  CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS — the deployed host's actual browser origins.

  Before #1624 this was rendered nowhere, so every deployed host ran with NO
  configured CORS policy and fell through to the two hardcoded origins in
  apps/worker/middleware/cors.ts — app.llamenos-hotline.org and
  demo.llamenos-platform.com, which belong to this project's own hosts and to
  no self-hoster. A self-hosted deployment's own SPA origin was not on its own
  allowlist.

  Note what SETTING it does, and why the list below must stay complete:
  cors.ts and lib/redirect-guard.ts both take their localhost-development
  branch only when ENVIRONMENT=development *and* this var is unset. Rendering
  it turns that escape hatch off — which is correct for a deployed host (an
  Ansible deploy is app_environment=production unless overridden), but it
  means anything the browser actually loads from has to appear here.

  Tauri's two origins are added by the app itself and are never listed here.

  The default is derived the same way every other consumer of the vhost
  layout derives it -- `api_subdomain` + `domain`, matching
  roles/llamenos-caddy/templates/caddy.j2's vhost blocks,
  playbooks/smoke-check.yml's `_api_host` and vars-production.example.yml's
  webhook_base_url. NOT app_domain/api_domain: those appear only in
  vars.example.yml, no role resolves api_domain at all, and on the production
  path both are undefined -- a default built on them would have produced an
  allowlist naming hosts this deployment does not serve.

  - api_subdomain set   -> https://api.<domain> (the API vhost) and
                           https://<domain> (which Caddy 301s to it)
  - api_subdomain empty -> https://<domain> alone (single-domain mode)
  - app_domain set      -> added too; templates/caddy.j2's updater vhost
                           already treats it as the app origin
#}
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS={{ cors_allowed_origins | default(
  (['https://' ~ api_subdomain ~ '.' ~ domain, 'https://' ~ domain]
     if (api_subdomain | default('')) | length > 0
     else ['https://' ~ domain])
  + (['https://' ~ app_domain] if (app_domain | default('')) | length > 0 else [])
  + (cors_allowed_origins_extra | default([]))) | unique | join(',') }}
{#
  METRICS_SCRAPE_TOKEN — the bearer token Prometheus presents to
  /api/metrics/prometheus. Unset, apps/worker/routes/metrics.ts falls through
  to requiring an authenticated admin holding `metrics:read`, which a scrape
  never is, so every scrape got a 401. Measured against a live deployment for
  #1624: roles/llamenos-prometheus also had the PATH wrong (/api/metrics is
  the admin-only JSON summary; only /api/metrics/prometheus has a scrape-token
  branch), so the app job collected nothing for two independent reasons. The
  same value is rendered into that scrape config — change it in one place.
#}
{% if (metrics_scrape_token | default('')) | length > 0 %}
METRICS_SCRAPE_TOKEN={{ metrics_scrape_token }}
{% endif %}
{#
  SIP_BRIDGE_URL — the sip-bridge sidecar as seen from inside the app
  container, i.e. the compose service name on llamenos-internal, NOT the
  loopback publish port (roles/llamenos-asterisk publishes it on
  127.0.0.1:<sip_bridge_local_port>, 3101 by default, for the host-side smoke
  probe only). The bridge's own listener always binds 3000.

  Unset, apps/worker/routes/health.ts's checkSipBridge returned null and the
  probe was omitted from /health/ready entirely — "not configured" scored
  exactly like "healthy", on a sidecar no call can route without (#1119).
#}
{% if llamenos_asterisk_enabled | default(false) | bool %}
SIP_BRIDGE_URL=http://sip-bridge:3000
{% endif %}
{#
  The per-volunteer SIP registrar and its TURN relay
  (apps/worker/telephony/registrar.ts, apps/worker/routes/webrtc.ts). Both
  TURN values must be present or /sip-token hands clients STUN-only ICE
  servers and an in-app call behind a symmetric NAT has no media path.
  SIP_REGISTRAR_SECRET falls back to HMAC_SECRET; set it only to rotate every
  volunteer's derived PJSIP secret independently of HMAC_SECRET.

  SIP_TLS_CA_FILE is deliberately NOT rendered here — see
  scripts/check-consumed-env.py's UNRENDERED_BY_DESIGN entry for it: the app
  container has no mount for the anchor roles/kamailio publishes, so a path
  would point at nothing inside the container.
#}
{% if (sip_registrar_secret | default('')) | length > 0 %}
SIP_REGISTRAR_SECRET={{ sip_registrar_secret }}
{% endif %}
{% if (turn_host | default('')) | length > 0 and (turn_secret | default('')) | length > 0 %}
TURN_HOST={{ turn_host }}
TURN_SECRET={{ turn_secret }}
{% endif %}
{#
  <PROVIDER>_WEBHOOK_IPS — the webhook IP allowlist (#1622). The var name is
  built at runtime from the provider name in middleware/webhook-auth.ts,
  middleware/webhook-ip-allowlist.ts and messaging/router.ts, so there is no
  fixed list of them; `webhook_ip_allowlists` is an operator map of
  provider -> CIDR list and each entry renders one line.

  Empty by default, and that is a deliberate limit on this change: #1609
  fixed this control's left-most-X-Forwarded-For bypass, but switching the
  control ON means pinning provider-published IP ranges in a template, and a
  range the provider later rotates away from silently rejects real webhooks —
  worse than the signature-only posture it replaces. #1622 owns that decision
  (fail closed, or an explicit opt-out so "unset" and "off" differ). What
  belongs here is the plumbing, so turning it on is an operator variable and
  not a template patch.
#}
{% for provider, cidrs in (webhook_ip_allowlists | default({})) | dictsort %}
{{ provider | upper }}_WEBHOOK_IPS={{ cidrs | join(',') if cidrs is not string else cidrs }}
{% endfor %}
ENVIRONMENT={{ app_environment | default('production') }}
HMAC_SECRET={{ hmac_secret }}
STORAGE_ENDPOINT=http://rustfs:9000
STORAGE_ACCESS_KEY={{ storage_access_key }}
STORAGE_SECRET_KEY={{ storage_secret_key }}
STORAGE_BUCKET={{ storage_bucket | default('llamenos-files') }}
{% if llamenos_transcription_enabled | default(false) | bool %}
WHISPER_URL=http://whisper:8080/v1/audio/transcriptions
{% endif %}
TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID={{ twilio_account_sid | default('') }}
TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN={{ twilio_auth_token | default('') }}
TWILIO_PHONE_NUMBER={{ twilio_phone_number | default('') }}
SERVER_SECRET={{ server_secret }}
{% if (webhook_base_url | default('')) | length > 0 %}
WEBHOOK_BASE_URL={{ webhook_base_url }}
{% endif %}
{% if (apns_key_p8 | default('')) | length > 0 and (apns_key_id | default('')) | length > 0 and (apns_team_id | default('')) | length > 0 %}
{#
  APNS_KEY_P8 is a multi-line PEM. A raw multi-line value breaks Docker
  Compose's env_file parser entirely (verified: "key cannot contain a space"
  on line 2 of the PEM, and the WHOLE .env file fails to load — not just this
  var). Compose's env_file parser DOES unescape literal \n inside a
  double-quoted value back into a real newline before it reaches the
  container's environment (verified with `docker compose config` and a real
  container run) — so emit it as one physical line, double-quoted, with
  embedded newlines replaced by the two-character sequence \n. No app-code
  change needed: process.env.APNS_KEY_P8 arrives with real newlines restored.
#}
APNS_KEY_P8="{{ apns_key_p8 | replace('\r\n', '\n') | replace('\n', '\\n') }}"
APNS_KEY_ID={{ apns_key_id }}
APNS_TEAM_ID={{ apns_team_id }}
{% endif %}
{% if llamenos_ntfy_enabled | default(false) | bool %}
NTFY_URL={{ ntfy_url | default('http://ntfy:80') }}
{# NTFY_URL is the server-side (internal) address. Android devices register
   endpoints on the PUBLIC vhost, and apps/worker/lib/ntfy-origin.ts trusts an
   endpoint only if its origin equals NTFY_URL or NTFY_PUBLIC_URL — without this
   line every device endpoint on https://<ntfy_domain> is rejected and Android
   devices can never be rung. #}
{% if (ntfy_domain | default('')) | length > 0 %}
NTFY_PUBLIC_URL=https://{{ ntfy_domain }}
{% endif %}
{% if (ntfy_auth_token | default('')) | length > 0 %}
NTFY_AUTH_TOKEN={{ ntfy_auth_token }}
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
{# ── In-app calling: the registrar, the relay, and the SIP edge's anchor ──
   Every variable below was consumed by apps/worker/ and assigned in NO Ansible
   template, so on a deployed host each silently took its absent branch and
   nothing reported it. roles/kamailio/tasks/main.yml carried a COMMENT saying
   an operator had to add them "by hand"; a comment is not a check, and nothing
   failed when the hand-step was skipped (#1657). #}
{% if llamenos_asterisk_enabled | default(false) | bool %}
{# SIP_REGISTRAR_SECRET is the master secret every volunteer's PJSIP endpoint
   password is derived from (HMAC_SIP_VOLUNTEER_SECRET over `vol_<pubkey16>` —
   apps/worker/telephony/registrar.ts `deriveVolunteerSipSecret`). Rotating it
   rotates every volunteer at once, which is why it is its own value and not
   HMAC_SECRET. Gated on asterisk because only our own PBX issues per-volunteer
   identities at all: every vendor path is refused at the source
   (`sipCredentialsMayBeIssued`, #1203), so on a vendor deployment this would
   be a secret nothing can use. #}
SIP_REGISTRAR_SECRET={{ sip_registrar_secret }}
{% endif %}
{% if llamenos_coturn_enabled | default(false) | bool %}
{# TURN_HOST/TURN_SECRET are what turn a STUN-only ICE set into one with a
   relay. apps/worker/routes/webrtc.ts returns undefined unless BOTH are set,
   and /api/telephony/sip-token then emits STUN only — a valid ICE
   configuration, a 200, and no media path for a volunteer behind a symmetric
   NAT (common on mobile carriers, i.e. exactly the volunteers this is for).
   TURN_SECRET must equal roles/llamenos-coturn's --static-auth-secret: the
   worker MINTS credentials under it and coturn VERIFIES them. Both render from
   `turn_secret`, so they cannot drift.
   Gated on the role that runs the relay, so these never name a host that
   refuses every allocation — a relay candidate that cannot work is worse than
   no relay candidate, because ICE spends its check budget on it. #}
TURN_HOST={{ turn_host }}
TURN_SECRET={{ turn_secret }}
{% endif %}
{% if (kamailio_enabled | default(false) | bool) and (kamailio_tls_enabled | default(true) | bool) and (kamailio_tls_publish_anchor | default(true) | bool) %}
{# SIP_TLS_CA_FILE is the SIP edge's public trust anchor, published to clients
   inside the authenticated /api/telephony/sip-token response so a volunteer's
   client can VERIFY a self-hosted PBX certificate that no public CA vouches
   for. It matters because liblinphone does not consult the platform trust store
   for its SIP socket — it verifies against its own CA set — so on both mobile
   clients this anchor is the only way that chain becomes verifiable at all.
   Absent means "verify against the device trust store"; it never means "do not
   verify".
   This is a CONTAINER path, and the mount that makes it readable is in both app
   compose templates (roles/llamenos-app/templates/compose/app.j2 and
   roles/llamenos/templates/docker-compose.j2). Rendering the path WITHOUT that
   mount is why #1656 declined to ship this: `readSipTlsTrustAnchor` would then
   log "not readable" and return undefined, which is strictly worse than the
   unset variable it replaced — the same outcome, plus an error line implying a
   broken anchor rather than an absent one.
   Both conditions matter: with publish_anchor false the role writes no anchor
   (correct for a publicly-trusted certificate, where pinning a leaf breaks at
   every renewal), so the path would name a file that is deliberately absent. #}
SIP_TLS_CA_FILE={{ sip_tls_anchor_container_path | default('/var/lib/llamenos/sip-tls') }}/sip-edge-anchor.pem
{% endif %}
{% if (glitchtip_dsn | default('')) | length > 0 %}
GLITCHTIP_DSN={{ glitchtip_dsn }}
{% endif %}
{% if llamenos_signal_enabled | default(false) | bool %}
SIGNAL_NOTIFIER_BEARER_TOKEN={{ signal_notifier_bearer_token }}
{% endif %}
{% if demo_mode | default(false) | bool %}
DEMO_MODE=true
DEMO_MODE_CONFIRM={{ demo_mode_confirm }}
{% if (demo_reset_cron | default('')) | length > 0 %}
DEMO_RESET_CRON={{ demo_reset_cron }}
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
{# ── Developer/test routes (/api/test-*) — THIRD of three independent layers ──
   DEV_ROUTES_ENABLED and DEV_RESET_SECRET open the destructive `/api/test-*`
   surface (reset the database, mint identities). They are ONLY ever wanted on
   a throwaway staging target that the end-to-end suite resets, never on a host
   serving real callers or volunteers.

   The `app_environment != 'production'` condition below is DELIBERATE, not an
   oversight: on a production host these two vars are silently omitted from the
   rendered .env even if an operator set `dev_routes_enabled: true` in vars.yml.
   The omission is safe to make quietly because it can only ever happen in a
   configuration two other layers already refuse outright — this layer exists so
   that a bypass of either of them still cannot write the backdoor to disk.

   The three layers, in the order a deploy hits them:
     1. RUN TIME, before any file is written —
        deploy/ansible/playbooks/tasks/guard-dev-routes.yml hard-fails the play
        when app_environment == 'production' and dev_routes_enabled or
        dev_reset_secret is set. Imported by preflight.yml, which both the
        hosted and the self-hosted deploy path run via setup.yml. Keyed on
        app_environment alone, with no reference to demo mode, so it survives
        demo mode's removal.
     2. RENDER TIME (here) — the var never reaches the .env on disk, so even a
        path that skipped the preflight guard (an ad-hoc role invocation, a
        future playbook that forgets the import) cannot template a backdoor onto
        a production host.
     3. PROCESS START — apps/worker/lib/config.ts throws when
        ENVIRONMENT=production and any of DEV_ROUTES_ENABLED / DEV_RESET_SECRET /
        E2E_TEST_SECRET is set, and apps/worker/lib/dev-surfaces.ts refuses to
        serve /api/test-* outside the ['development', 'staging'] allowlist (and,
        outside development, without a >= 32-char secret).

   `staging` (and any other non-production value the var set allows) still emits
   both vars normally — that is the whole point of #723: the E2E suite cannot
   bootstrap a deployed target that answers 404 on /api/test-*.

   deploy/ansible/scripts/check-required-env.py renders this template at
   app_environment=staging AND app_environment=production with identical
   dev_routes_enabled/dev_reset_secret inputs and asserts the opposite outcomes,
   so neither half of this conditional can rot unnoticed. #}
{% if (app_environment | default('production')) != 'production' %}
{% if dev_routes_enabled | default(false) | bool %}
DEV_ROUTES_ENABLED=true
{% endif %}
{% if (dev_reset_secret | default('')) | length > 0 %}
DEV_RESET_SECRET={{ dev_reset_secret }}
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
